I’d make the card container non-interactive and expose a real, labelled button for its action. At the same time, replace inline literals with the existing Tailwind tokens and use a wrapping fluid layout.

```tsx
type CardProps = {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate: () => void;
  actionLabel?: string;
};

export function Card({
  children,
  onActivate,
  actionLabel = "Open card",
}: CardProps) {
  return (
    <article className="w-full max-w-5xl border border-brand bg-surface p-6 text-ink shadow-sm">
      <div className="flex flex-wrap gap-6">
        <div className="min-w-0 max-w-full grow basis-96 text-lg">
          {children}
        </div>

        <aside className="min-w-0 max-w-full grow basis-60 text-sm text-ink/60">
          sidebar
        </aside>
      </div>

      <button
        type="button"
        onClick={onActivate}
        className="mt-6 bg-brand px-4 py-2 text-surface shadow-sm focus-visible:outline-2 focus-visible:outline-offset-2 focus-visible:outline-brand"
      >
        {actionLabel}
      </button>
    </article>
  );
}
```

How this maps to the findings:

- `accessible_interaction_risk`: removes `onClick` from the `<div>` and uses a native `<button>`. That supplies keyboard activation, focusability, and button semantics automatically. A visible `actionLabel` provides the accessible name, so patching the `<div>` with `role`, `tabIndex`, and custom key handlers is unnecessary. If the action is navigation, use an `<a href>` instead.

- `design_token_escape`: replaces raw colors, RGB shadow, spacing, and font sizes with the configured `brand`, `surface`, and `ink` colors plus Tailwind’s spacing, typography, and shadow scales. If the original pink or grey values are intentional brand choices, they should first become named tokens in `tailwind.config.ts`, then be referenced through utilities.

- `responsive_fragility`: replaces the `800px`/`720px` widths and fixed height with `w-full`, a tokenized maximum width, and a wrapping flex layout. Tokenized `basis-*` values establish preferred proportions but can shrink or wrap on narrow containers. `text-lg`/`text-sm` use root-relative units instead of fixed pixel typography.

`crimes context` also reported no related files or likely tests. I’d add a focused component test asserting that the action is discoverable by role and activates with Enter/Space, plus a narrow-viewport browser test checking for horizontal overflow. Then rerun `crimes context src/Card.tsx --format json` to confirm all three finding types clear.
